All 54 teams originally registered for qualification was held in two rounds like in the previous editions in 2019 and 2021. [[Réunion national football team|Réunion]] and [[Zanzibar national football team|Zanzibar]] were not full members of the CAF and were therefore excluded from participation. [[Eritrea national football team|Eritrea]] withdrew after the first round draw. [[Kenya national football team|Kenya]] and [[Zimbabwe national football team|Zimbabwe]] were suspended by FIFA at the time of the second round draw and were excluded from the competition after their suspensions were not lifted in time.

In [[2023 Africa Cup of Nations qualification preliminary round|the preliminary round]], the twelve lowest-ranked teams in the [[FIFA Men's World Ranking|FIFA world rankings]] of December 2021 competed against each other in a knockout system with two legs. The six winners of the preliminary round and the remaining 42 higher-placed teams were drawn into twelve groups of four in April 2022. The second round was played from June 2022 to September 2023 in a double round format. The group winners and runners-up from all twelve groups, with the exception of [[2023 Africa Cup of Nations qualification Group H|Group H]], qualified for the final round. Apart from the hosts from [[Ivory Coast national football team|Ivory Coast]], only one other team qualified from Group H.

===Qualified teams===
The following teams qualified for this edition of the tournament with no debutant nation for the first time since [[2015 Africa Cup of Nations|the 2015 edition]]. Seventeen teams that participated in the most recent edition in 2021 returned for the event.

[[DR Congo national football team|DR Congo]], [[South Africa national soccer team|South Africa]], [[Angola national football team|Angola]], [[Namibia national football team|Namibia]], and [[Tanzania national football team|Tanzania]] made their return to the Africa Cup of Nations after missing out on the [[2021 Africa Cup of Nations|2021 edition]]. [[Zambia national football team|Zambia]] made its return after an almost nine-year absence from the event. [[Mozambique national football team|Mozambique]] made its fifth appearance after a fourteen-year absence.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/africa/67545224 | title=Africa Cup of Nations 2023 Groups D, e and F preview | work=BBC Sport }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.cafonline.com/caf-africa-cup-of-nations/news/mozambique-seal-afcon-return-after-dramatic-win-over-benin/ | title=Mozambique seal AFCON return after dramatic win over Benin | date=9 September 2023 }}</ref>

The [[Comoros national football team|Comoros]] failed to qualify after making their debut in 2021, whereas [[Kenya national football team|Kenya]] and [[Zimbabwe national football team|Zimbabwe]] were disqualified due to their suspension from FIFA.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://kick442.com/last-time-wonder-comoros-to-miss-afcon-2023/ | title=Last time 'wonder' Comoros to miss AFCON 2023 | date=17 June 2023 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://theconversation.com/kenya-and-zimbabwe-have-been-suspended-from-world-football-what-happens-next-178040 | title=Kenya and Zimbabwe have been suspended from world football: What happens next | date=March 2022 }}</ref> [[Sudan national football team|Sudan]], [[Malawi national football team|Malawi]], [[Gabon national football team|Gabon]], [[Sierra Leone national football team|Sierra Leone]], and [[Ethiopia national football team|Ethiopia]] also failed to qualify after appearing in the 2021 tournament.

